From dadcb37019c58416e0b4c079394d64e7a989327e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yohan <you@example.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 10:03:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Frontend: fix scanner crash and missing ML/AI/recommendations
 data

ROOT CAUSE (all three issues trace to the same bug): emailMapper.ts's
mapApiEmailToUiEmail() assumed every caller passes the flat
ApiEmailSummary shape from GET /api/v1/emails (the list endpoint), with
top-level sender/threatScore/classification/riskLevel fields. But
ScannerPage.tsx (and only ScannerPage.tsx) calls getEmail() right after
scanning, which hits GET /api/v1/emails/:emailId - a structurally
different, full-detail shape with no top-level sender at all, and with
score/level/classification nested under risk, ML under mlAssessment,
AI under aiAssessment, and Batch 5C's recommendations under
recommendations.

Confirmed via a live backend trace (POST scan -> GET detail) that the
backend pipeline is fully correct: risk.score, mlAssessment.probability
(real trained tfidf-logistic-v1 model, not a stub), explanations, and
Batch 5C's recommendations were all present and correct in the raw API
response the whole time. Nothing was lost in analysis/generation on the
backend - only in this one frontend mapping function.

Issue 1 (crash): apiEmail.sender.split('@') with sender undefined on
the full-detail shape threw exactly the reported "Cannot read
properties of undefined (reading split)", caught by ScannerPage's
try/catch and shown as "Analysis Failed".

Issue 2 (missing ML/AI/threat score/explanations): masked by the
Issue-1 crash (result never rendered), and even without the crash the
mapper never read risk.*, mlAssessment, aiAssessment, or explanations
at all, nor did ScannedEmail have fields to carry them.

Issue 3 (missing recommended actions): Batch 5C generates and returns
recommendations on GET /api/v1/emails/:emailId exactly as designed -
confirmed live. The frontend simply never read that field or had
anywhere to put it.

Fix (frontend only, no backend/API-contract changes):
- emailMapper.ts: mapApiEmailToUiEmail now derives every field through
  a safe fallback chain across BOTH the list-summary and full-detail
  shapes (sender, subject, threatScore, classification, riskLevel,
  spf/dkim/dmarc, whyFlagged from explanations), instead of assuming
  one shape and calling .split() unguarded
- mockData.ts: added optional ScannedEmail fields (mlProbability,
  mlStatus, aiSummary, aiStatus, attackType, recommendedActions) plus
  a RecommendedAction type mirroring Batch 5C's Recommendation shape -
  additive/optional, so no existing ScannedEmail literal needs to change
- api.ts: added ApiEmailDetail/ApiRecommendation types documenting the
  actual GET /:emailId shape (kept getEmail()'s own return type as
  implicit any, unchanged, to avoid rippling into unrelated callers
  like ForensicsPage's separate local type)
- ScannerPage.tsx: scan result card now shows ML probability, AI
  summary, attack type, and recommended actions when present, and
  explicitly labels AI/ML as unavailable-fallback (not fake data) when
  no provider ran - never presents an UNAVAILABLE status as a genuine
  result

Validated against a real captured backend response (live scan of a
phishing-style .eml -> GET detail): all fields map correctly, the
crash no longer reproduces, and the old list-summary shape still maps
identically (no regression).

npm run build: clean. tsc --noEmit: only pre-existing, unrelated
ForensicsPage.tsx errors remain (not touched, not introduced by this
change).
